  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The wrinkled thimble morel (Verpa bohemica) is a spring mushroom belonging to the morels and allies family, characterized by its distinctive bell-shaped or thimble-like cap. Despite its common name suggesting it's a true morel, it differs in several key ways and is considered a false morel by many mycologists.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap attachment",
      "description": "The cap hangs freely from the stem like a bell or thimble, attached only at the top rather than extending down the stem",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap texture",
      "description": "The cap surface is wrinkled or brain-like rather than having the honeycomb pits of true morels",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem structure",
      "description": "The hollow stem is typically whitish or pale and may be chambered or chambered at the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Wrinkled thimble morels are found in moist woodlands, near streams, and in disturbed areas throughout New England, fruiting in early spring (March-May).",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Some people eat wrinkled thimble morels, but they can cause gastrointestinal upset in sensitive individuals and should always be thoroughly cooked, unlike true morels which are safer to consume.",
